snippet.textDisplay:
@rmac3217 The left have usually been the most vocal against the richest people, often saying that no humans should legally be allowed to become too rich. Hence why they support heavier taxation on the richer people get, as well as a limit to how big a business can become. The more far left people who are closer to being communist believe that employers shouldn't be able to take such a large percentage of money that their workers have made for the company, then paying the workers a tiny percentage. Many with those beliefs think that the workers should all own a share of the company, so all the money a worker makes goes back into their pocket, and they get a say in how the company should be led. Having worked in a lot of industries, I don't trust people to agree on a good way to run the company, and everyone would just turn against eachother over time, or crash the company. Note I was using the label communist loosely, as what I described is more just generally anti-capitalist, unionist practices. I can support workers being paid a greater percentage of the money they make for the company, but I am distrusting of unions and especially against communism.

The right wing on the other hand, sees that a single person can make themselves immense riches with enough effort and luck, and would rather a society of winners and losers if it means they have a chance at turning themselves rich. It seems a common belief is if a person truly put in enough effort in the right areas, they will be rewarded with wealth. I do like the idea of having your fate in your hands, and I respect the amount of money and work it takes to start a business and manage to make real money from it, with the risk of losing it all hanging over your head. Ultimately it seems the game is largely rigged though and it has to be needlessly cruel to certain kinds of people for the current system to exist. For the ultra rich to exist, the terribly poor also need to exist, a permanent underclass needs to be exploited. Look at Dubai for example, without desperately poor people willing to work all day every day for basically nothing, the whole place would fall apart.

On a side note, I remember over a decade ago when "automation" was the hot-topic rather than AI. The articles would claim as machines take over most of our work load, workers will only have to work something like half a day, 3 days a week, and likely earn even more money than they do now. People were amazed at the idea, when now it's become obvious a worker won't be paid for the work a machine does for them, the bosses will just pocket that money and the workers will be lucky to have a job at all. It's amazing how much the tone has shifted since those days. It would only make sense if the extra profits generated allowed for enough extra tax dollars to be collected so the government can pay people for the work that is no longer available, but the extra prosperity being generated. It seems like a joke to imagine such a thing in the USA especially. I think government regulation needs to be applied to AI and its place in industries before it all becomes monopolies. Ethics and safety in new sciences are always a top priority for consideration and lawmaking, yet we don't see that with AI from any government bodies.

